WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.920
It wasn't just digital de-aging that made&nbsp;
Harrison Ford look forty years younger in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.600
the opening of "Indiana Jones and the Dial of&nbsp;
Destiny" — crew members also made a special mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.580
that lives firmly in the uncanny valley. Stick&nbsp;
around for a closer look at this creepy creation.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.140
Prior to the release of "Indiana Jones and&nbsp;
the Dial of Destiny," there was a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.040
talk about how the film would use de-aging&nbsp;
technology for the sequence at the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.900
of the film set during World War II. The goal&nbsp;
was to make Indiana Jones look similar to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.220
he did in "Raiders of the Lost Ark," and&nbsp;
the digital effect was mostly a success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.720
While there's ample CGI on display throughout&nbsp;
the rest of the movie, there are also good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.700
old-fashioned practical effects — including a&nbsp;
realistic mask that may just haunt your dreams.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.400
On Instagram, special effects artist Waldo Mason&nbsp;
shared images of a stunt mask used in "Dial of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.720
Destiny." While the realistic skin texture&nbsp;
makes the mask somewhat uncanny on its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.700
it doesn't seem all that freaky — but it's a&nbsp;
different story when we see it in action. Mason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.500
included two pictures of a stunt double wearing&nbsp;
the mask atop a motorcycle and standing in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.660
office. The use of stunt doubles with prosthetics&nbsp;
is nothing new for action movies — but out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:11.100
context, the mask is definitely getting a&nbsp;
strong reaction from Indiana Jones fans.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.240
There's been no shortage of pop culture&nbsp;
similarities pointed out on social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.720
Several people compared it to Michael&nbsp;
Myers' mask from the "Halloween" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.180
and one Twitter user even wrote —

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.460
"[It] definitely looks like&nbsp;
something from Dawn of the Dead."

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.120
Other comparisons included the&nbsp;
realistic disguises of the "Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.280
Impossible" franchise and the mask that Ryan&nbsp;
Gosling wears at one point in "Drive." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.600
the most common reference seems to have been&nbsp;
to the "Prank Show" sketch from Netflix's "I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.200
Think You Should Leave," which depicts&nbsp;
star Tim Robinson covered in so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.340
prosthetics that he loses his cool in a&nbsp;
shopping mall. Many fans of the show were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.540
quick to point out the Indiana Jones mask's&nbsp;
resemblance to the troublemaker Karl Havoc.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.858
"I'm not doing it… I don't even&nbsp;
want to be around anymore."

00:01:53.858 --> 00:01:56.940
Despite all the jokes, some film&nbsp;
enthusiasts pointed out that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.220
were happy to see practical effects being&nbsp;
used in 2023. Another Twitter user stated —

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.240
"Long proud history of these in&nbsp;
movies. I'll take these over CGI any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.180
day because at least the brain knows&nbsp;
there's a real person in the shot."

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.260
It's great to see stunt performers&nbsp;
continuing to get work in movies of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.980
this scale — even if Harrison Ford thinks&nbsp;
they could back off every now and then.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.520
Stuntmen and stuntwomen play&nbsp;
crucial roles on film sets:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.020
they often do the difficult scenes an actor&nbsp;
can't risk doing, as an injury would shut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.700
down production. With Harrison Ford being&nbsp;
an octogenarian, there's little doubt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.360
stunt performers were needed occasionally&nbsp;
to help with some of his scenes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.460
Ford still did plenty on his own and even thought&nbsp;
the stunt workers were sometimes doing too much.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.300
When speaking with Esquire, Ford&nbsp;
discussed one scene in which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.440
thought too many people were trying to help&nbsp;
him off of a horse. As he described it —

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.620
"I look down and there's three stunt&nbsp;
guys there making sure I [don’t] fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.160
off the stirrup. They said, 'Oh, we&nbsp;
were just afraid because we thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.300
you know,' and bah bah bah bah. And I said,&nbsp;
'Leave me the f— alone, I'm an old man.'"

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.160
Ford also said that he wanted to actually look&nbsp;
like an older man getting off a horse — after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.120
Indy's advanced age is a pretty important&nbsp;
part of his story in "Dial of Destiny."

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.920
A lot of hard work goes into making a movie.&nbsp;
Someone needs to make a convincing Harrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.780
Ford mask, someone else needs to wear that&nbsp;
mask, a third person has to shoot it in action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.180
and a whole team of people have to fix any present&nbsp;
issues in post. It's all for the greater good of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.560
bringing a story to the big screen, even if&nbsp;
the mask looks so hot and uncomfortable that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.700
the person wearing it would probably want to&nbsp;
rip it off while having an existential crisis.

